    sampleAnswer: `Two slots. The running image stays in A while the new one is written to B; power loss there costs nothing. Then verify B's CRC against its header, and only then write the boot flag selecting B, as a single word in its own sector, so that write is the one atomic-ish step. On reset the bootloader re-validates B: header magic and its own CRC, length inside the slot, hardware ID, body CRC or signature, and that word 0 points into RAM and word 1 into the slot with the Thumb bit set. Then it jumps with MSP and VTOR set. If B boots but hangs, the watchdog resets it; B never called confirm, so the bootloader counts the failure and falls back to A and reports it. The watchdog stays serviced during the update too. The bootloader's own sector is write-protected and never erased, there's a forced-entry path through a button or a magic RAM word, and below that the STM32 ROM bootloader on BOOT0 is the last resort.`,
