    sampleAnswer: `They're rungs of one ladder. Model in the loop tests the control model alone; software in the loop runs the real code compiled for a PC against a simulated plant, which proves logic but not timing or I/O; processor in the loop runs it on the target MCU, which adds the real compiler, execution time and fixed-point effects; hardware in the loop connects the real controller, with its real I/O, electrically to a rig that runs the plant model in real time and drives real voltages, currents and bus frames. HIL is where you catch a mis-wired ADC channel, a loop that overruns when CAN traffic peaks, and whether an open sensor is detected in time, because the fault-insertion unit can open or short any wire on command. What HIL cannot prove is anything the plant model leaves out, so the model is validated against real measurements and a final real-plant layer remains.`,
