    sampleAnswer: `Bus-off. Each failed transmission adds eight to the transmit error counter and at 256 the node leaves the bus; 32 consecutive failures at 100 milliseconds is about three seconds, which matches. I'd read the error status register over the debugger first: TEC 256, receive counter 0 and the bus-off flag says its own frames failed and nobody else's did. Then termination with power off: 60 ohms across the pair is healthy, 120 means one terminator is missing, which was the loose far-end connector; cold made the mismatch worse. Then a scope on a frame at the sample point to see the ringing. Fix the terminator, verify 60 ohms, and add recovery in firmware, because on most controllers a bus-off node stays off until software asks, after it has seen 128 times 11 recessive bits. And a bus-health counter in diagnostics so the next harness fault is a log entry, not a dead dashboard.`,
