    sampleAnswer: `An array of sixty-four bytes, a head written only by the interrupt and a tail written only by the loop, both masked with sixty-three on increment. Put: compute next head; if it equals tail the buffer is full, return false and let the caller count an overrun; otherwise store the byte, then advance head. Get: if tail equals head it is empty; otherwise read, advance tail. Capacity is sixty-three because head equal to tail must mean empty. No lock is needed because each index has one writer, the data is written before head is published, and an aligned byte or word store on Cortex-M is atomic, so the other side sees the old or the new index and never a torn one; a stale read just makes the put refuse a byte it could have taken or the get miss one it will get next time. If two interrupts wrote to it that argument dies and I'd need a critical section or one ring per source. Size: at 115200 baud a byte every 87 microseconds, times the longest the loop can be away, say two milliseconds, is twenty-three bytes, so sixty-four with margin, and I'd log the high-water mark to confirm.`,
