    sampleAnswer: `In CubeIDE I'd pick the Nucleo board so the LED, button and ST-LINK UART are pre-mapped, confirm PA5 as output and USART2 asynchronous, and read the clock tab: HSI 16 megahertz divided by M sixteen is one megahertz into the PLL, times N three-thirty-six is a three-thirty-six megahertz VCO, divided by P four is eighty-four megahertz SYSCLK, with APB1 halved to forty-two. Generate, and read what came out: MX_GPIO_Init sets the GPIOA enable bit in RCC AHB1ENR and then read-modify-writes MODER, OTYPER, OSPEEDR and PUPDR for pin five; HAL_GPIO_WritePin is a single store to BSRR, the set-reset register, so it is atomic. SystemClock_Config programs PLLCFGR, waits for PLLRDY, raises the flash wait states first and then switches CFGR to the PLL and waits for SWS to confirm, and reprograms SysTick for a millisecond tick. I'd add a _write function so printf goes through HAL_UART_Transmit, use an elapsed-time loop instead of HAL_Delay so nothing blocks, flash and debug over SWD with a breakpoint on the toggle, watch ODR change in the peripheral view, and put a scope on the pin: a millisecond or two of error is the tick granularity, and the HSI's one percent is why I'd move to HSE for serious UART work.`,
