    id: "clock-tree-walk",
    moduleId: "mcu",
    topic: "Clocks",
    question: `A PWM output set up for 20 kHz measures 40 kHz, and a UART on the same board prints garbage. Walk the clock tree and the pin configuration to the causes.`,
    probes: ["What clocks a timer on APB1?", "Which register tells you the clock the chip is really running?", "What would a scope on MCO show?"],
    rubric: {
      strong: [
        "Timer rule: on a bus with prescaler not 1 the timer clock is twice the bus clock, so a 42 MHz APB1 clocks TIM2 at 84 MHz; the PWM arithmetic used the wrong input.",
        "UART garbage: checks the pin's MODER is alternate function with the right AF number, then the baud against the bus clock; HSI's ±1 % and the baud-tolerance budget.",
        "Verifies with RCC->CFGR SWS and the RDY flags, HAL_RCC_GetSysClockFreq computed from registers, and SYSCLK on MCO measured with a scope.",
        "Walks the tree in order: source, PLL ranges, SYSCLK with flash latency, HCLK, APB1/APB2 limits, peripheral enables.",
      ],
      weak: ["Blames the timer peripheral.", "Trusts SystemCoreClock as truth.", "Cannot say what alternate function mode does."],
    },
    sampleAnswer: `Factor of two on a timer says clock input. On the F4, timers on a bus whose prescaler is not one run at twice the bus clock, so APB1 at forty-two megahertz feeds TIM2 at eighty-four; the prescaler and period were computed for forty-two. I'd confirm by reading RCC CFGR in the debugger: SWS says what is really driving SYSCLK, PPRE1 says the bus divider, and then recompute. For the UART I'd first look at the pin: MODER must be alternate function and AFRL must select AF7 for USART2, otherwise the pin is driven as a plain output while the USART toggles nothing; then the baud register against the real bus clock, and I'd remember that HSI is one percent off, which with the wrong bus clock is garbage and with the right one is inside the tolerance budget. Finally I'd put SYSCLK on the MCO pin and scope it so the frequency is a measurement, not a belief.`,
    relatedLesson: "gpio-and-the-clock-tree",
    industries: [
      { sector: "automotive", note: "Clock-tree configuration is a reviewed artefact in ECU projects; a wrong divider ripples into every timing requirement." },
      { sector: "industrial", note: "Motor-control PWM at the wrong frequency can damage hardware; verifying the timer clock with an instrument is a commissioning step." },
      { sector: "medical", note: "Pump and sensor timing derive from the same tree; a verified clock measurement appears in the design verification record." },
    ],
