    sampleAnswer: `Each frame I take the mid-value of the three channels, so one wild channel cannot move the output, which an average would not guarantee. Each channel's value is compared with the voted value. If it differs by more than the miscompare limit, or the data is missing, the channel is flagged for that frame. A persistence counter counts consecutive flagged frames and isolates the channel at, say, three, resetting on any good frame so one glitch does not remove a healthy channel. The limit must be larger than normal disagreement, sensor accuracy and sampling skew, and the persistence trades nuisance isolations against how long a real fault stays in the loop. In a simulation I ran, a channel stuck from frame 6 was flagged at frame 7, once the signal had moved past the limit, and isolated at frame 9, with the output never more than a tenth of a unit from the truth. With two channels left that disagree, the voter cannot tell which is right, so it goes fail-passive: holds the last good value, reports it, and leaves the decision to the wider system. It also cannot see faults inside its limit, or a fault that alternates good and bad frames.`,
