    sampleAnswer: `A task is a function with its own stack, a priority and a state: Running, Ready, Blocked or Suspended. On every tick, usually 1 ms, the kernel increments the tick count, wakes any task whose delay has expired, and runs the highest-priority ready task, preempting the running one if a higher one is ready. The scheduler also runs whenever a task blocks or an interrupt wakes a task. Equal priorities can share time by slicing, and when nothing is ready the idle task runs. Higher-priority tasks are never delayed by lower ones, so when the processor is overloaded the lowest-priority task gets only what is left and misses its deadlines, while the important tasks look healthy. In a simulation I ran, making a 3 ms logger take 7 ms pushed utilisation to 1.08 and caused ten misses in 240 ms, all in the logger. For periodic work I use rate-monotonic priorities, shortest period highest, and vTaskDelayUntil so the period does not drift.`,
