    sampleAnswer: `A hardware-in-the-loop rig keeps the unit under test and its real electrical interfaces and simulates the plant, in my case the aircraft and its sensors, as a model running in real time. It has four parts: a real-time target that runs the model at a fixed step, I/O boards that turn model values into real voltages and bus frames and read the unit's outputs back, a fault-insertion stage that can open, short or corrupt a signal, and a host that starts scenarios, injects faults and records everything. The bench is read from the interface control document row by row: each signal gives a source, a bus interface, a load or a measurement point, plus the test-only rows the ICD lacks. Safety comes from interlocks that act on their own: a supply current limit, a hardware emergency stop, an over-temperature trip on the load. What it cannot tell you is bounded by the model, because the unit is proven against the model of the aircraft, and by the delay the rig adds, the model step plus the conversions, which I would measure and put in the report. I would also say plainly whether I used the rig or built it.`,
