    sampleAnswer: `I would write the contract first: bytes before a start byte are ignored, a length of zero or above the maximum is rejected, a bad CRC is never delivered, the parser never writes outside its buffer, and after any rejection it resynchronises so a good frame hidden inside a bad one is still found. The code is a four-state machine: wait for start, length, payload, CRC, with every byte since the start kept so a rejected frame can be replayed. Each sentence gets a test. The two that people forget are the boundary and the resync: a maximum-length frame with a guard byte after the buffer, because an off-by-one that stores the CRC at index equal to length changes no delivered frame and only the guard sees it; and a corrupt outer frame containing a good inner frame, because dropping the whole frame loses it. After a rejection I replay from just after the failed start byte and never trust the damaged length. The replay costs stack and work per byte, so I would measure that on the target, and I would note that an eight-bit CRC lets about one corrupt frame in 256 through.`,
