    sampleAnswer: `A control loop measures, compares with the setpoint, computes a command and acts, over and over. The error is setpoint minus measurement. P is proportional to the error now: fast, but on a first-order plant it settles at Kp over one plus Kp of the setpoint, so Kp of two gives two thirds and it never reaches the target. I adds up the error over time, so the command keeps rising until the error is zero, at the cost of overshoot: in my simulation Kp one, Ki four overshot by about twenty percent, and Kp three, Ki three did not. D reacts to the trend and damps overshoot, but it divides measurement noise by the time step, and in my simulation it made the command about twenty times jumpier. When the actuator hits its limit the integral keeps growing, which is windup, and the output overshoots afterwards; freezing the integral while the command is clamped fixed it. A simulation shows what each term does, not how an aircraft behaves, and a gain change means I re-collect the step response with the gain version recorded.`,
